	'M3-G1':
		"`scene.animateProperty` moves one value of a light, camera, material or object from keyframes, and the glTF loader turns a file's `KHR_animation_pointer` channels into property tracks of its clips: a light's values, a material's values, a node's visibility, and the offset and scale of a base color or emissive texture's transform. Every map keeps a transform of its own, as three.js keeps one for each texture: `packages/engine/src/scene/materials.test.ts` reads the material's row and checks that one map's transform reaches its own slot and no other, and `packages/engine/src/scene/scene.test.ts` plays two tracks of one material through the real writer and reads the rows each map received, which is the check that a shared transform fails. `packages/engine/src/scene/gltf-properties.test.ts` checks each pointer's map slot and its own base value. `tests/image/gltf-properties.spec.ts` plays four of the five Khronos animation-pointer samples, AnimatedColorsCube, LightVisibility, CubeVisibility and AnimationPointerUVs, at five times each, and CubeVisibility at six, and matches three.js r186 with `@needle-tools/three-animation-pointer` within 1e-5 on the animator's sampled values. The large sample loads from the sample content. PotOfCoalsAnimationPointer moves only two texture rotations, which neither engine moves, so it has no value to compare. `examples/property-animation` moves a lamp, a material and a camera in code; D-209 records the design, the transform that each map keeps, and the camera gap",
