	'M3-V9':
		"Far trees draw as cards that cast the crown's shape: a coverage function on a custom material, tested by a fragment stage in a caster on both paths, with a still-caster routing that keeps the far cascades cached and a stored depth that moves a card's own shadow lookup off its plane. The turned-copy light is fixed, so a card the sun sees from behind a turned tree lights as its mesh does, and `impostor-turn-azimuth` is the case the old test could not show. The card field of 152 cards, measured with its coverage function, its shadow offset and its drawn back face, costs 1.02 ms on WebGPU, 1.02 ms in compatibility mode and 1.34 ms on WebGL2 at 1600 x 900 on this Mac's GPU at High, 7.38 and 9.43 ms on the iPad Pro 13 at Medium at 1032 x 1264 with 2x pixels, and 4.39 and 4.26 ms on the Galaxy S25 at Low at 360 x 621 with 3x pixels; the three-view blend costs 0.15 to 0.20 ms on the Mac, up to 0.53 ms on the phone, and no measurable amount on the iPad's WebGPU path. `impostor-bake` bakes a conifer, a birch and an oak at each preset's grid, and all twelve bakes take 3.16 s on the Mac, 1.96 s on the iPad Pro 13, 2.54 s on the Galaxy S25 and 3.97 s on the Galaxy S24; the three species take, conifer then birch then oak, 177, 44 and 60 ms at Low up to 924, 241 and 342 ms at Ultra on the Mac, 112, 39 and 41 up to 535, 155 and 217 ms on the iPad, 150, 36 and 50 up to 740, 200 and 283 ms on the S25, and 293, 54 and 72 up to 1,158, 297 and 426 ms on the S24. The two atlases take 819,200 bytes at 5 by 5, 1,605,632 at 7 by 7, 2,654,208 at 9 by 9 and 4,718,592 at 12 by 12, so a preset's atlas is 800 KiB to 4,608 KiB and the plan's 1.6 MB to 9 MB estimate is twice that, because the bake packs four bytes a texel and not eight. The still-caster routing is witnessed by the batch test that the card claims `castsStill`, the render test that a still-flagged card is not a moving caster, and `a_still_card_in_a_far_cascade_leaves_its_layer_cached` on both builders, which counts the far cascade's draws over eight frames: a still card draws it as often as a caster that never moves, and a moving card draws it in every frame; `materials.test.ts` holds that the public `castsStill` option sets the core flag. The turned hand-over is witnessed twice, and the two are not the same guard. `impostor-handover`, whose band now holds turned pines, is the picture CI runs, so it is what fails when a turn is ignored. `impostor-handover-strip.spec.ts` compares each card with the crown of its turned mesh — a card turned 60 degrees further sits farther from the mesh and differs from the correct card — but its whole file is skipped unless `NULL3D_IMPOSTOR_STRIP_COST` is set, so CI does not run it and it is the owner's quantified look bar rather than the guard. The `impostor-frame` plan times the demo that draws the cards at each preset a path draws: the iPad Pro 13 holds every D-241 forest ceiling it has, from 15.62 ms against 15.6 at Low to 50.19 against 71.1 at Ultra on WebGPU, and the Galaxy S25 holds its 30 Hz display at every preset with 19.73 to 28.05 ms of GPU time on WebGPU; D-241 has no S24-class WebGPU row and no Mac row, and the Mac holds its 120 Hz display at every page with 4.19 to 10.15 ms of GPU time. The figures are in D-274 and the run files of `.dev/tested-devices/`.",
	'M3-L4':
		"Terrain blocks and plant leaf cards morph toward their next coarser level before the engine switches, so a switch draws no pop. Both add-ons write a four-number vertex record that their own custom material's vertex offset reads: the terrain stores the parent grid's height and its block's size and height box, and the leaves store each vertex's place at the next level and that level's error. The record fills the vertex slot that morph targets use, so the engine gains one public input, `input.vertexRecord`, and no vertex location; a mesh takes `targets` or `morphTargets`, never both. `tests/image/terrain-morph.spec.ts` and `tests/image/leaf-switch.spec.ts` hold the camera still and move each switch across it, so the only change between two frames is the hand-over. On this Mac's GPU the terrain's hill moves 0.026% of the frame with the morph off and 0.000% with it on, and the leaf cards 0.634% against 0.036%, which is 94% of the switch removed; compatibility mode reads 0.026% and 0.321% to 0.023%, WebGL2 0.035% and 0.528% to 0.030%, with the morph on leaving none and a tenth of it. Both specs pass on all three GPU paths with `./node_modules/.bin/playwright test --project=chrome-real-gpu terrain-morph leaf-switch` from `tests/`. The unit tests hold the parent grid's triangulated heights, the kept and dropped card targets, each level's targets naming the level after it, the record's staging and update at the vertex slot, the refusal of `targets` with `morphTargets`, and that the card material's surface stays equal to the plain plant material's. The judged GPU check passed over the branch's own commits, `028abe3f8..25e0bd6bd`: s4 medium 2.76 to 2.54 ms, s6 medium 3.90 to 3.88, s4 high 3.45 to 3.55 and s6 high 5.80 to 5.63, every page the same, where a page fails only above 25 per cent, above 0.3 ms and above twice its noise. The terrain demo draws with the block morph on a phone and a tablet: the terrain plan passed 9 of 9 pages on the iPad Pro 13 in Safari 26.6.1 and 9 of 9 on the Galaxy S25 in Chrome 152 at `842d32e59`, recorded in `.dev/tested-devices/ipad-pro-13-safari/20261029-112415-terrain.md` and the same folder of the phone, whose timing the runner marks unreliable because its display ran at 30 Hz. D-273 records the design, the measurements, and the two faults the measurements found: the block's corner distance over-morphed it at its own split, and the leaf shader measured the tree from the camera-relative matrix position, which morphed the cards a level early",
	'M3-R12':
		"A production build compiles only the shader permutations its pages draw. The build takes `--permutations <file>`, one whole permutation word per line, with the device bits left out so one list serves every device module; `?permutations=record` makes the drawing thread record the word of every variant a pipeline asks for, and the recorder drives every demo in hold mode at the low and high presets on WebGPU and WebGL2, then live, pressing every button of a showcase's controls HUD. It wrote 172 words to `examples/shader-permutations.txt`, which `examples:build` reads. On this Mac in quiet windows with cold caches: every word compiles 78,491 builds in 420 s of compile and 446 s of wall clock, writing 3,481,695,669 bytes in 6,852 files; the listed words compile 4,189 builds in 23 s and 25.4 s, writing 181,076,198 bytes in 479 files. The showcases' production build falls from 3,718,479,532 bytes in 7,965 files to 415,238,908 bytes in 1,174 files. Two faults were found and fixed on the way: the saved lookup threw on a missing word while a first-use module was still loading, which would have broken skinning, bloom and every first-use feature; and reading the list from the main generated module pulled start shader data into the page bundle and the sketch worker, by 48.4 and 16.2 per cent, so the list now lives in a module of its own. On the narrowed set, 183 demo pictures pass, the three showcases pass 9 of 9 across the three GPU paths, four controls-HUD tests pass with features turned on at run time, and 182 crate tests pass including one that the committed list builds 4,189 of the 78,491. A page that gains a feature after recording is not in the list, and the engine fails at the pipeline that needs it, naming the word and saying to record again. D-276 records the design",
